pkg.description:
C3 application package this Type System element _belongs_ to; note that this maybe different from the package
where this type-system element was declared.

@see #package

name.description:
The name of the primitive type (such as `double` or `boolean`). This is usually the same as the DSL syntax,
except it does not include variations such as (`date` or `datetime with millis`) or modifiers (`!` and `?`).

@see #keyword

enumRefType.description:
This declares that the values are constrained to those defined on the named enum type. The fields of the enum
type must have the same type as the primitive type which is constrained by them.

```type
enum type Interval { SECOND MINUTE HOUR }

type X {
  interval: string enum Interval
}
```

@see Enum

title:
Primitive values are basic objects such as `int` and `boolean` as well as some more complex objects such as
`datetime` and `json`. The simpler ones are represented by primitives in programming languages and the more complex
ones using library objects where available.

In all languages, primitives are implemented with built-in primitive values. Most of these have natural mappings
except for DateTime which always has C3 semantics and is implemented custom for each language.

| C3       | JavaScript  | Python     | Java       |
|----------|-------------|------------|------------|
| binary   | ArrayBuffer | _various_¹ | ByteBuffer |
| boolean  | Boolean     | bool       | boolean    |
| byte     | Number      | int        | byte       |
| datetime | DateTime²   | DateTime²  | DateTime²  |
| decimal  | Number      | float      | BigDecimal |
| double   | Number      | float      | double     |
| float    | Number      | float      | float      |
| int      | Number      | int        | long       |
| int16    | Number      | int        | short      |
| int32    | Number      | int        | int        |
| bigint   | BigInt      | int        | BigInteger |
| json     | _various_³  | _various_³ | JsonNode   |
| string   | String      | string     | String     |

¹ Primitive binary values in python are represented by a value which implements the
{@link https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/buffer.html buffer protocol}, including and limited to instances of  `bytes`,
`bytearray`, and `memoryview`.

² Full implementation of C3 datetime semantics.

³ A mixture of native objects representing the JSON structure naturally.

